      "writtenOn": "2024-01-10T16:53:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e What I have here matches the request/responses defined in realtek\u0027s data sheet\n\nBut those request/responses are specific to the chip right? Could we just embed those within the chip specific emulator code so the test doesn\u0027t have to know about it?",
      "parentUuid": "de986abb_7caad734",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 14,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 53
      },
      "revId": "e91db55f623a8f7d1cdcd8b0ffe291f57b21cccc",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b36ae397_0e9f514d",
        "filename": "zephyr/include/emul/emul_pdc.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 14,
      "author": {
        "id": 1530058
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-10T17:07:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The PDC API follows the UCSI spec, which Realtek loosely follows.  Any other chip that follows UCSI spec can reuse these APIs.  They\u0027ll just need to massage the data for how their chips use it.  Mainly I\u0027m trying to validate \n\n```\nUCSI data IN -\u003e emulator encodes to chip specific representation -\u003e driver decodes I2C data -\u003e UCSI data OUT\nVerify IN \u003d\u003d OUT\n```",
      "parentUuid": "c7c30386_b0573075",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 14,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 53
      },
      "revId": "e91db55f623a8f7d1cdcd8b0ffe291f57b21cccc",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f85bf6f0_409875d5",
        "filename": "zephyr/include/emul/emul_pdc.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 14,
      "author": {
        "id": 1425418
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-10T17:17:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ahh, so I suppose the question is should the test know the UCSI spec?\n\nIMO that\u0027s more of a driver level abstraction and not something the test needs to know in order to create test declarative scenarios. Keith, do you think this is too abstract?",
      "parentUuid": "b36ae397_0e9f514d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 14,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 53
      },
      "revId": "e91db55f623a8f7d1cdcd8b0ffe291f57b21cccc",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
